{#
    One card, and the title in it is the link.

    A card wrapped in one anchor announces its whole contents as that link's
    name, and takes selecting the text inside it away from the reader. So what
    is pressable is the line that says where pressing it goes, and the card
    follows on hover — which is what makes the whole thing feel like the target
    it deliberately is not. Where there is nowhere to go, the title is a title.

    This is the markup `sds-teaser` renders, node for node: a renderer and an
    element writing the same class differently is the drift the class layer
    exists to prevent.
#}
<article class="sds-teaser">
    <div class="sds-teaser__body">
        {%- if node.option('title') %}
            <h3 class="sds-teaser__title">
                {%- if node.option('to') -%}
                    <a href="{{ renderLink(node.option('to')) }}">{{ node.option('title') }}</a>
                {%- else -%}
                    {{ node.option('title') }}
                {%- endif -%}
            </h3>
        {%- endif %}
        <div class="sds-teaser__text">
            {%- for child in node.children -%}
                {{ renderNode(child) }}
            {%- endfor -%}
        </div>
    </div>
</article>
